{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact head c5cf189f88aa75ea74855cac96e8decf1275e2e9, all 56 declared artifacts, packages/ui/README.md first.\n\nArchitecture: replacing the preview's approximation with the policy runtime, sharing glob and carry logic, centralizing landing facts, retaining historical note anchors, guarding explain requests, and exposing partial-read coverage are sound changes. The main-view reproductions in review 82f2743b are repaired. Two remaining projection gaps prevent approval.\n\nSecurity: workspace tokens remain neither requested nor displayed. Compromise aborts and unexpected-main states are correctly distinguished in landing cards and the admin queue. The live activity feed still infers forward pushing from an expected-main read without knowing whether the op has an abort; that contradicts the safety exception even when the loaded op contains it.\n\nSimplification: use the existing validatePolicy function before draft replay, and describe unresolved log events using only facts the event establishes. Neither repair needs a new endpoint, expression-profile feature, or transport.\n\n1. P1 — The live feed and explanation title still promise forward retries after a compromise abort (src/room/live/describe.ts, publication-unresolved; live-room.ts load/explain). For every expected-main publication-unresolved event, describeEntry says the room pushes the same reserved commit forward again. That event contains only op and readBack, with no abort fact. A valid sequence can record an abort while a push is still unresolved, then read expected main; forward pushes are stopped, although the old push might still land. The independent test builds a LiveRoom whose loaded unresolved LandOp has abort.tokenRevoked:true and whose log has that expected-main event. The feed still promises forward pushing while LandingDetail correctly says it stopped. Make the event sentence factual: main read as expected and publication remains unresolved. If describing recovery, qualify it with known abort state for that operation; with a truncated history or no op, do not assume there was no abort. Apply the same rule to explain titles. Test abort-before-first-unresolved, truncated history with a loaded abort, ordinary complete-forward, and unexpected main. This is the remaining part of review 82f2743b's publication-copy finding.\n\n2. P2 — A successful dry run can show a policy that cannot be adopted (src/room/dryrun.ts compileDraft/dryRun; mock/mock-room.ts dryRun). The compiled document is not passed through validatePolicy. Independent tests exercise five drafts through MockRoom.dryRun: require-review with existing ID security-review; require-review with path src/[ab].ts; a default dependency area src/[ab]/**; a global input src/[ab].ts; and refuse-claim with existing ID security-review. validatePolicy rejects each resulting document for a duplicate ID or invalid glob, but the dry run returns status:replayed. These inputs are reachable through the unrestricted rule-ID/path fields. The screen then presents a runtime prediction and an export fragment for an invalid configuration; duplicate IDs can also hide new obligations because the comparison uses obligation IDs. Validate the entire compiled document before evaluating any history, return the validation problems and a fix without a prediction, and cover all four draft kinds. Keep the faithful preview/export equivalence and the explicit refusal of unsupported refuse-claim target shapes.\n\nValidation: repository typechecking passed; all 71 candidate component/adapter tests and all six Playwright tests passed, including the end-to-end scenario and keyboard navigation. The two independent local diagnostic tests passed after selecting the correct unresolved scenario fixture; they demonstrate the five invalid-draft cases and the feed/loaded-abort contradiction. I reviewed all ten committed screenshots and the revised source and declaration-generation setup. One nonblocking verification issue: the phone screenshot test waits for an obligation but not the asynchronous diff. My rerun wrote a 390x2858 partial proposal image, whereas the committed image is 390x6842 with the full diff. Wait for a loaded diff element before capturing, as the laptop screenshot test already does, so mobile review consistently covers the whole proposal. No deployment or live credential call was made.\n\nThe unsupported-target warning and displayed overlap mismatches are useful limits of the refuse-claim compiler; the compiled runtime result and preview now agree on the tested broad, quoted, and non-ASCII cases. The known live-read gaps remain explicit, and this review does not require a production transport or extra endpoint.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:de6843e1979c1270d04f0f93d96df0218c26c9c7","binding":"assigned","head":"c5cf189f88aa75ea74855cac96e8decf1275e2e9","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9df858e0103d2d2f588397b2d254d30a9b083153\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:20cf77e0d8e8478ea9044953ed5903b698055dc1\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5fe797b1448dff83c03513a4a265fca8a68654cc","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}